from write_dossiers import add,NOTES
import json
n=json.loads(NOTES.read_text('utf-8'))
q=n['rxr']
q['problem']='RxR reduces R2R shortest-path and length biases and provides multilingual dense grounding. It contains 126,069 spoken and transcribed instructions in English, Hindi and Telugu over 16,522 paths, plus guide and follower pose traces. It directly tests whether agents follow route constraints instead of only reaching the goal.'
q['method']='Sections 3–4, PDF pp3–6: sample a simple sequence of rooms, then find the shortest panorama path constrained to that sequence. Greedy selection favors coverage and paths that are not globally shortest. In total, 44.5% of paths are not globally shortest; the average is 14.9m and 27.4% longer than the shortest route. Spoken instruction timestamps are aligned through ASR/transcript dynamic time warping to six-degree-of-freedom poses. Human followers validate instructions and supply alternative paths. Primary metrics nDTW and sDTW measure path adherence because endpoint success alone is insufficient. The baseline in Section5, p7 uses multilingual BERT word features, a CNN text encoder, an LSTM policy and EfficientNet-B4 image features. Training mixes 50% behavioral cloning and 50% policy-gradient rollouts with incremental nDTW reward. The grounding auxiliary loss is L(z,m)=log Σi exp(zi)−log Σi mi exp(zi), equal to the negative log of attention mass on observed features, where binary mi labels regions viewed by the human. An analogous mask applies to words. Training uses 100k steps, batch32 and Adam at 1e−4. Inference uses the full instruction and panorama; human traces supply supervision rather than test inputs.'
q['novelty']='Multilingual spoken instructions, dense pose alignment, varied paths that need not be shortest, and human alternative paths jointly strengthen the benchmark. The dataset offers supervision for language progress, but its own attention-supervision results are mixed. Annotation availability does not imply solved grounding.'
q['results']='AUTHOR EXPERIMENT: Table4, p6: following the correct first step and then going straight achieves 27.2% SR on R2R versus 13.7% on RxR, supporting reduced shortcut bias. Table5, p8: combining guide and follower paths improves monolingual training; joint multilingual training and automatic translations can hurt. Visual attention supervision has mixed effects and textual attention masking did not improve performance. Table6 shows weak direct transfer across datasets despite shared buildings. VERDICT: RxR is an essential control for route-faithfulness proposals. Report languages and guide/follower training conditions, and distinguish English subsets from the full multilingual benchmark.'
q['implementation']='The official data repository is pinned. The release README, formats and available assets were inspected. The paper implements its baseline in VALAN; inspecting the data repository does not establish inspection of VALAN model code. Dataset alignment and metric definitions are direct primary evidence.'
q['bottlenecks'][0]['description']='Dense human alignment is partial and imperfect; simple attention constraints do not consistently improve policy performance.'
q['bottlenecks'][0]['evidence']='Section5 grounding-supervision experiments and Table5'
q['bottlenecks'][1]['description']='Multilingual and cross-dataset distribution differences persist despite more data and shared scenes.'
add('rxr',**q)
q=n['reverie']
q['problem']='REVERIE requires navigating to and identifying a remote object described by a high-level command, rather than merely stopping near a location. Object recognition and stopping are coupled: correct navigation alone is insufficient.'
q['method']='Section3, PDF pp4–5: 21,702 instructions refer to 4,140 target objects in 489 categories. Target boxes are projected from 3D object annotations into simulator views and retained within 3m. The agent receives panoramic RGB and navigable viewpoints and may output one target box or object ID. Selecting the correct supplied candidate, or predicting a box with IoU at least0.5, counts as correct grounding. Multiple viewpoints may see the same object. The baseline combines FAST-short navigation with MAttNet subject, location and relation matching. For each view, the top three matching objects supply averaged visual features and BiLSTM-encoded category labels, concatenated with panorama features. Navigation uses action cross entropy and progress squared error. The pointer uses margin ranking: L=Σ[δ+S(o,r−)−S(o,r)]+ +[δ+S(o−,r)−S(o,r)]+, where positive object/expression pairs must outscore mismatched pairs by margin δ. Printed Eq8 has a negative squared-error term and uses logit notation inside a logarithm; the released loss must disambiguate these rather than implementing the literal expression. Section5.1 describes training the pointer first, then using its object information for navigator training, qualifying the compact joint-loss description.'
q['novelty']='A remote referring-expression benchmark and interactive navigator–pointer baseline couple navigation with object grounding. The task goes beyond goal proximity. However, supplied annotated object boxes are privileged perception proposals compared with detected boxes in a physical deployment.'
q['results']='AUTHOR EXPERIMENT: Table3, p8 reports test joint success11.28% versus7.07% for FAST plus a pointer and77.84% for humans; navigation-only SR is19.88%. Table4 places the model at the true goal: MAttNet achieves48.98% grounding accuracy versus90.76% for humans, showing that object selection remains difficult even after navigation succeeds. The weak language-only baseline demonstrates a need for visual evidence, but does not prove every model uses every semantic relation. VERDICT: report RGS/RGSPL alongside SR/SPL, and distinguish ground-truth boxes from detected boxes. Goal-only datasets cannot replace this evaluation.'
q['implementation']='The pinned official release includes the navigation model, trainer and eval_release.py. modelFast.py and follower.py were retrieved for the loss, stopping and grounding audit. Reproduction additionally requires the dataset, simulator and trained perception components.'
q['bottlenecks'][0]['description']='Navigation success can hide incorrect object identity, while annotation-provided proposals simplify perception.'
q['bottlenecks'][0]['evidence']='Section3 metric definitions and Table4 grounding at the true goal'
q['bottlenecks'][1]['description']='Goal-directed instructions may omit route evidence, and multiple objects or viewpoints create ambiguity. Endpoint confidence is not object confidence.'
q['bottlenecks'][1]['evidence']='Dataset definition and measured pointer errors'
add('reverie',**q)
q=n['topologicalplanning']
q['problem']='Separate semantic global planning from low-level control using a topological map built by the agent. Each environment is explicitly explored along ten trajectories before instruction execution. Mapping uses RGB-D panoramas, heading and ground-truth odometry; navigation subsequently uses learned localization.'
q['method']='Section3, PDF pp3–6: sparsify and merge exploration graphs using reachability estimates. Nodes store globally oriented panoramas; edges encode eight directions and three distance bins. A message-passing GNN encodes nodes, edges and global context, followed by map, language and cross-modal Transformer layers. The planner selects a node sequence y with p(y|G,L)=Πt p(yt|y<t,G,L); learned trajectory-position embeddings distinguish selected and unselected nodes. Node/STOP classification uses cross entropy and AdamW. A separately DAgger-trained controller receives previous, current and subgoal panoramas and predicts one of24heading sectors plus a scalar distance. A Gaussian bias toward the previous direction suppresses oscillation from perceptual aliasing. Low-level actions turn15degrees or move0.25m. Predicted distance determines when to advance to the next planned node. The full plan is computed before execution, distinct from online exploration.'
q['novelty']='Cross-modal global planning on an automatically constructed topology is combined with modular visual localization and control. Crucial stopping prior art appears in Section4.1, p6: categorical STOP is compared with a separate binary-cross-entropy STOP head jointly trained with node classification (CMTP-BCE), repeated-node stopping, and a no-STOP control. Binary stop supervision alone is therefore not a new contribution for2027.'
q['results']='AUTHOR EXPERIMENT: Table1, p6: on unseen scenes with agent-generated maps, categorical stopping achieves26.0% SR and separate BCE27.3%; on R2R graphs the corresponding values are28.7% and30.2%. The no-STOP oracle success rates are64.0% and60.5% after20planning steps. That control adds exploration and does not isolate softmax normalization as the cause of stopping errors. Table2, p7 shows that heading hysteresis improves control: unseen agent-generated-map success rises82.5→89.6 with variance3. VERDICT: useful planner, controller and stopping ablations, with pre-exploration and sensor assumptions that prevent direct comparison to unknown-scene, single-run DUET.'
q['implementation']='The official project https://topovln.stanford.edu/ exposes map assets and an email contact for code instead of a public repository. No email was sent. No executable source or commit was inspected. This is a bounded search limitation, not a claim that no implementation exists.'
q['bottlenecks'][0]['description']='Stopping and map-quality errors persist even with a pre-explored topology. Separate binary cross entropy helps modestly but does not close the no-STOP oracle gap.'
q['bottlenecks'][0]['followups']=['DUET online topological navigation','HSGM double confirmation']
q['bottlenecks'][1]['description']='Pre-exploration, true mapping odometry and a fixed initial plan move substantial costs and uncertainties outside instruction-time planning.'
q['bottlenecks'][1]['followups']=['CM² online map prediction','IVLN persistent memory']
add('topologicalplanning',**q)
